/-!
# Newman (2024): *When Arguments Merge*

A head bears Merge features, and a phrase merging with it checks every unchecked feature it can
and at least one. Features may go unchecked, and they belong to categories: V bears `[·D·]`,
checked by a DP, and `[·X·]`, checked by any phrase, and v bears these with `[·V·]` and `[·wh·]`.
A phrase checking a subset of the features another checks must then merge first when both merge
with one head. Among the operations possible at a step of a derivation, Weak Economy prefers one
checking more features, unless either would bleed the other.

This file formalizes [newman-2024]'s theory of feature-driven Merge and four of its applications:
the complement position of non-DP arguments, the binding pattern of the dative alternation, agent
focus in Mayan subject questions, and the subjects of passive ditransitives. The last three are
fitted to the examples of `Data.Examples.Newman2024`.

Merge with Feature Maximality is a selection discipline (`Minimalist.Selection`) on sets of
features, beside the discipline in which a head selects from an ordered stack. A head then
selects a sequence of constituents exactly when each lies outside the closure of those before it
(`selects_iff`), the closure in which an item is covered by those that check every Merge feature
it checks, and the order of a head's arguments follows from that closure.

## Main definitions

* `Selector`, `Phrase.checks`: the features of a head, and those a phrase checks.
* `Selection (Finset Selector)`: Merge with Feature Maximality as a selection discipline.
* `mergeFeatures`, `categorial`: the features a category bears, and the licensing of syntactic
  objects in which a lexical item has the features of its category.
* `closure`, `Selects`: the closure the features of a head induce on lexical items, and a
  sequence of constituents a head selects in order.
* `Matched`: the constituents each check a feature of their own.
* `System`, `System.Run`: the operations at a head, and a sequence of them each checking a feature
  still unchecked; `System.Derivation` is a run under Weak Economy, and `System.runs` is the
  finite set of the runs from a state.
* `selectionHead`, `configuration`: the head function of Merge as selection, and the binding
  theory, m-command without reverse c-command.
* `IsDitransitive`, `Binds`: a vP that Merge licenses from a ditransitive's arguments, and binding
  between its objects under a word order.
* `specifiers`, `AgentFocus`, `verbForm`, `HasVerbForm`: the specifier sequences of a transitive
  vP, Voice and T cross-referencing one argument, the form of the verb that results, and the
  forms of a clause with given fronted arguments.
* `high`, `low`, `HasSubject`: the operations at v in a ditransitive whose indirect object is in
  v's complement or in V's, and the subjects such a clause can have.

## Implementation notes

* A phrase's checkable features are read off its category and the features it bears, and a
  lexical item checks those of the phrase it heads, bearing `[wh]` where its feature bundle does.
  No lexical item bears the object-marking feature or values `[uφ]`, which the operations of the
  later sections check.
* The selection state of a syntactic object checks features from the bottom up, so it follows
  the order of Merge only where a later constituent is higher. The first specifier merged is
  outermost, by Paillé's generalization of Richards's tucking in, so the sections on specifiers
  check features along the order of the operations (`System`) and build the tree from it.
  M-command is Barker and Pullum's command relation generated by the maximal projections, and
  case is Marantz's dependent case.
* An operation is possible when it checks a Merge feature of an accessible phrase, and a
  derivation ends only when nothing possible remains, as Preminger's obligatory operations
  require. Weak Economy does not compare two operations when either would bleed the other, as in
  the analysis of Greek wh passives, where wh-movement of the indirect object bleeds clitic
  doubling and clitic doubling checks more features.
* The trees of the dative alternation are planar syntactic objects. The daughter that projects
  at a node is the sister that selects the other (`selectionHead`), and the maximal projections
  that m-command reads are those of that head function. `IsDitransitive` takes every vP whose
  heads select their arguments with the direct object at V and the subject at v, a VP specifier
  on either side. That is seven trees (`isDitransitive_iff`): the book draws three, and not the
  four with the subject as v's complement or VP outermost. The binding prediction holds on all
  seven.
* A language of the Mayan type moves the object to check v's `[·D·]` before the subject merges
  wherever that leaves the subject licensed (`Realizes`), and its `[·wh·]` stays active once
  checked. An operation applies at most once. The agent-focus form is read off a row's gloss
  line, as is a Greek clitic.
* The last-resort model of Coon, Mateo Pedro and Preminger is the one formalized in
  `Studies/CoonMateoPedroPreminger2014.lean`, with a determinerless object as its Caseless
  object.

